    "turn_idx": 14,
    "turn_text": "Well, as you recall, cures was a bill. It was pretty all encompassing bill. It, of course, had the research titled a title that was supposed to help with getting things from the laboratory bench to the bedside in a more straightforward fashion, perhaps holding down cost along the way. So we probably should have paid a little bit more attention to that. The other aspects of it, D.S. Tim Murphy at the time, had offered a significant mental health bill and that became a mental health title. And then the interoperability bills that I had worked on for several years became the third component, the interoperability title. And in fact, the last Congress, Ralph Chairman of the Health Subcommittee, we sort of did hold a hearing, had several hearings, two hearings on how the scientific development side was going. I also had a hearing on the mental health title and had a hearing on the interoperability title. It did take forever for the Office of National Coordinator for Health Information Technology to sort of digest that information that was in the Interoperability Title of the Cures bill. They did finally get their rule published. In fact, I think that was the last hearing that I chaired as Health Subcommittee Chair in December of 2018. Dr. Rucker, who's the head of the off the national coordinator continue to work on that now they did put things on pause during the pandemic. I hope we're at a place where now we can move beyond that because clearly the interoperability part of this is still I mean the criticality of that is not gone away and it began the bottom line is is it going to be helpful to patients and the answers I believe it is and if that is the case then we need to get on with it.",
