    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "And I guess one other aspect and we certainly can't forget it is our alliance on our broadband network. You know, it was called into service in a way that I don't think anybody anticipated. So if you think of it as a national massive stress test on our national broadband network and for the most part, it passed. It delivered what was needed and when it was needed. But we do understand that there are gaps to fill. There are places that are under underserved, certainly the capacity at times was strained and the whole discussion around now the build out of the 5G network nationally. I think that discussion has been advanced considerably when you look at just how dependent we were upon our broadband networks to get us through the pandemic because sure it's telehealth and you don't want the network to go down when you're in the middle of a critical telehealth discussion with the patient and sure it was distance learning and you don't want, I mean, It's tough enough shape that kids are losing so much of the learning opportunity from the end of last school year in the beginning of this one, but you don't want to child school day to come to an end because there wasn't the broadband capacity and the, of course, the telework that is being done and people that are. Successfully completing their tasks online rather than in person because it is safer for them. You want that to be able to continue. So it's really defined, you know, one of the other jurisdictions are committing is to help communication subcommittee. And it is really the criticality of that aspect of what our committee work is was clearly put on display during the pandemic.",
