  {
    "episode_id": 71078,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71078.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "We have had a number of hearings on this over the years. I will tell you, I don't think any one of us has all the answers, but there was a rather jarring story in my local newspaper, the house warning news a week or so ago about the first attributable death to ransomware hacking, where some poor woman's data was sequestered because of a ransomware attack and it was felt to be injurious enough that she didn't survive whatever the underlying event was. So clearly, it is being able to provide the type of information that's going to flow across the network, particularly in the telehealth space, but being able to do that safely and securely and not be vulnerable to people who want to steal data or people who just want to hijack it. So it is critically important. I will also tell you that there was an individual rock to my attention. They said, you know, our daughter has some significant medical problems, some of which could perhaps cause her to have an episode of a loss of consciousness. She's out in public. I'd like to be someone to be able to pick up her phone and know about her and not have that data behind the passwords and whatnot when she wouldn't be able to provide them. you know, raise the idea of perhaps, you know, a medical, like a medical alert bracelet that people would wear that would have their drug allergies, their blood type. Perhaps there is a way to incorporate that into someone's personal device to have a guest walled off from other aspects of what they carry on their phone, but at least some of the critical medical data that someone might otherwise have carried in a medical or bracelet.",
