  {
    "episode_id": 71078,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/1/71078.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "You know, one that stands out in my mind and what do you think about it? Of course, I've had the career in public service. Yes, I've had the career in health care, but anybody who goes through medical school and as I did go through graduate school first and then through medical school, you also have a significant educational career. And it was toward the was in my senior year in medical school. My medical school was relatively new. It was, and this was back in the 70s, of course, so it's no longer new, but it was a pass fail system. So there wasn't, you know, we always hear about medical students are always gunning for the top grade point average to try to lift some of that burden from the student population. They said, we're going to be on pass fail. There is also at the end of your medical career. There is an honorary medical society called Alpha Omega Alpha. Their motto is worthy to serve the suffering. There was a lot of consternation in our class. You know, how in the world, usually it's the top 10% of the class that's picked for AOA. How are they going to do that with us being on a pass-fail system? So somehow they worked out with professorial recommendations. But the end of the story was I was selected to receive one of the AOA slots that year. And it was, for me, personally significant because my dad had been an AOA. And it was not just the affirmation of what I had accomplished so far in my life. It was also the direction for what was yet to come in my life and to live up to that motto, worthy to serve the suffering. That is something I think about every day.",
